1. Title: Titanic - Machine Learning from Disaster

2. Sources:
    (a) Creator: Kaggle Dataset Team
    (b) Donor: Kaggle Community
    (c) Date: 2012 (continuously maintained and used)
    (d) URL: https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic

3. Past Usage:
    This dataset is one of the most well-known introductory datasets in machine learning, commonly used to illustrate binary classification problems.
    It has been featured in countless tutorials, competitions, and educational materials to teach:
        - Data preprocessing and cleaning
        - Feature engineering
        - Model selection and evaluation
    Common algorithms used include:
        - Logistic Regression
        - Decision Tree, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting
        - Support Vector Machine (SVM)
        - Neural Networks (MLP)

4. Relevant Information:
        --- The Titanic dataset records detailed information on 891 passengers aboard the RMS Titanic, 
            which sank in 1912 after colliding with an iceberg. Each record describes personal and 
            travel-related attributes such as age, gender, passenger class, fare, and family members aboard.
        --- The task is to predict whether a passenger survived (1) or not (0) based on these attributes.
        --- This dataset serves as a complete example for understanding the end-to-end workflow of a 
            machine learning project — from data cleaning to model evaluation.

5. Number of Instances: 
        --- Training set: 891 rows
        --- Test set: 418 rows

6. Number of Attributes: 12 total features, including the target variable Survived

7. Attribute Information:
        Attribute	 |  Description	                                         |  Type	 |  Notes
        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        PassengerId	    Unique ID for each passenger	                        Integer	    Not used for modeling
        Survived	    Survival indicator (0 = No, 1 = Yes)	                Integer	    Target variable
        Pclass	        Passenger class (1 = Upper, 2 = Middle, 3 = Lower)	    Integer	    Related to socioeconomic status
        Name	        Full name of the passenger	                            String	    Useful for extracting titles 
                                                                                            (Mr, Mrs, Miss, etc.)
        Sex	            Gender	                                                String	    male / female
        Age	            Age of the passenger	                                Float	    Contains missing values
        SibSp	        # of siblings/spouses aboard	                        Integer	    Used to build FamilySize
        Parch	        # of parents/children aboard	                        Integer	    Used to build FamilySize
        Ticket	        Ticket number	                                        String	    Often excluded from modeling
        Fare	        Ticket fare	                                            Float	    Should be normalized or scaled
        Cabin	        Cabin identifier	                                    String	    Many missing values
        Embarked	    Port of embarkation                                     String	    Some missing values
                        (C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton)	
        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------            

8. Missing Attribute Values:
        Attribute	     |  Approx. Missing (%)	 |  Recommended Handling
        --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        Age	                ~20%	                Fill with median or group-based median by Sex & Pclass
        Cabin	            ~77%	                Drop or extract first letter as category
        Embarked	        <1%	                    Fill with mode (‘S’)
        --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

